            <div xml:id="d1038">

                <pb facs="1084" n="964" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg964"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">853.00 Revolution 1931/5: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Portugal</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >South</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-04-09T11:00:00-05:00">April 9, 1931—11
                    a.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>8. Your telegram No. 7, April 8, 6 p.m. While this Government is happy to note
                    the efforts which are being made toward a peaceful <pb facs="1085" n="965"
                        xml:id="pg965"/> settlement and earnestly hopes that bloodshed may be
                    averted, in view of this Government’s well-established policy of
                    non-interference in European affairs, I believe it would be unwise for the
                    American Government even to aid in mediation as you suggest, since, with a
                    similar offer by the British, a controversy between the Portuguese Government
                    and the revolutionary forces might immediately arise as to which of these two
                    offers should be accepted and there would be inevitable taking of sides on that
                    subject, with the consequent identification of the American Government with one
                    side or the other.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Consul Funchal.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
